2. Trapping Rain Water:

Code:
class Solution {
    public int trap(int[] height) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = height.length - 1;

        int leftMax = 0;
        int rightMax = 0;

        int water = 0;

        while (left < right) {

            if (height[left] <= height[right]) {

                if (height[left] >= leftMax) {
                    leftMax = height[left];
                } else {
                    water += leftMax - height[left];
                }

                left++;

            } else {

                if (height[right] >= rightMax) {
                    rightMax = height[right];
                } else {
                    water += rightMax - height[right];
                }

                right--;
            }
        }

        return water;
    }
}

Approach

Use the two-pointer technique while maintaining leftMax and rightMax, the maximum heights seen from both sides. Process the side with the smaller height because the trapped water there is determined by that side's maximum boundary.

Complexity
Time: O(n) — each element is processed at most once.
Space: O(1) — only pointers and maximum-height variables are used.

